      "text": "To solve this problem, we start by using the fact that \\(\\alpha\\) is inversely proportional to \\(\\beta\\). This relationship can be expressed mathematically as:\n\\[\n\\alpha \\beta = k\n\\]\nwhere \\(k\\) is a constant. \n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\alpha = 4 \\text{ when } \\beta = 9\n\\]\n\nFirst, we substitute these values into the equation to find \\(k\\):\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 9 = k\n\\]\n\\[\nk = 36\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(\\alpha\\) when \\(\\beta = -72\\). Using the same constant \\(k\\) (\\(k = 36\\)), we set up the equation:\n\\[\n\\alpha \\cdot (-72) = 36\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(\\alpha\\):\n\\[\n\\alpha = \\frac{36}{-72}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha = -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\alpha\\) when \\(\\beta = -72\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\]",
